---
/**
 * The MCP catalogue, and what a harness has to know to call it.
 *
 * Every tool, description, schema and method on this page comes out of
 * `src/generated/mcp.json`, which scripts/mcp.mjs lifts from the compiler's
 * own `MCP_TOOLS` literal and dispatch. The help text is the recorded one from
 * `records/cli.json`.
 *
 * This page is the contract. The recorded loop, where an agent actually calls
 * these tools and the output is a transcript, is /agents/. Nothing here is a
 * measurement and nothing here ran.
 */
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";

import mcp from "../../generated/mcp.json";
import cli from "../../../records/cli.json";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const verb = cli.verbs.find((entry) => entry.name === "mcp");
if (!verb) {
  throw new Error(
    `tools/mcp: records/cli.json has no "mcp" verb, so this page cannot show ` +
      `what the server says about itself. Re-record with npm run record:cli.`,
  );
}

interface ToolArgument {
  name: string;
  type: string;
  required: boolean;
  description: string | null;
  html: string | null;
}

interface Tool {
  name: string;
  description: string;
  html: string;
  command: string;
  optional: string[];
  line: number;
  arguments: ToolArgument[];
  schema: string;
}

const tools = mcp.tools as unknown as Tool[];

/* The verb's help is no longer printed here: the catalogue is what a harness
 * sees, and the help text is on the CLI page with every other verb's. What is
 * kept is the check that the verb exists, because a `records/cli.json` with no
 * `mcp` verb means the compiler stopped serving this page's subject. */

/* The methods, minus the notification arms, which carry no id and want no
 * answer: a harness implements those by doing nothing. */
const requests = mcp.methods.filter((entry) => !entry.method.startsWith("notifications/"));
const notifications = mcp.methods.filter((entry) => entry.method.startsWith("notifications/"));
---

<Page
  kind="TechArticle"
  title="MCP tools"
  summary="iyi mcp serves the compiler's own verbs as Model Context Protocol tools over stdio: check, fix, context, test and doc, each one a shell around the same binary. The catalogue, the schemas, and the two LSP methods beyond the protocol."
>
  <section class="wrap head">
    <p class="label">Tools, agents</p>
    <h1>The compiler, as a toolbox an agent can hold.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <code>iyi mcp</code> serves {tools.length} tools over stdio. Each one
      runs this same binary with arguments the command line already takes, so
      what a harness gets back is what a shell would have gotten. This is the
      literal the server sends at <code>tools/list</code>, lifted from{" "}
      {mcp.catalogue.source}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <ul class="tools">
      {tools.map((tool) => (
        <li class="tool">
          <div class="tool-head">
            <h2><code>{tool.name}</code></h2>
            <span class="runs">
              {tool.command}
              {tool.optional.length > 0 ? ` [${tool.optional.join(" ")} ...]` : ""}
            </span>
          </div>
          <p class="sub" set:html={tool.html} />
          <table class="args">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">argument</th>
                <th scope="col">type</th>
                <th scope="col">required</th>
                <th scope="col">what it is</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {tool.arguments.map((argument) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><code>{argument.name}</code></td>
                  <td>{argument.type}</td>
                  <td>{argument.required ? "yes" : "no"}</td>
                  <td set:html={argument.html ?? ""} />
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Pointing a harness at it</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      There is no client stanza in the repository to quote and this page will
      not invent one: a configuration file belongs to the harness. What a
      harness needs is the command, the transport and the methods.
    </p>
    <dl class="evidence wiring">
      <dt>command</dt>
      <dd>{mcp.transport.command}</dd>
      <dt>transport</dt>
      <dd>stdio, one JSON-RPC message per line, both directions</dd>
      <dt>protocol version</dt>
      <dd>the client's, and {mcp.protocol.version} when the client names none</dd>
      <dt>requests</dt>
      <dd>{requests.map((entry) => entry.method).join(", ")}</dd>
      <dt>notifications</dt>
      <dd>{notifications.map((entry) => entry.method).join(", ")}</dd>
      <dt>anything else</dt>
      <dd>
        answered <code>{mcp.unknown.code}</code>, <code>{mcp.unknown.message}</code>
      </dd>
      <dt>beyond LSP</dt>
      <dd>
        {mcp.custom.map((method, i) => (
          <>
            {i === 0 ? "" : ", "}
            <code>{method.method}</code> runs {method.delegates}
          </>
        ))}
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="sub">
      So a harness that speaks LSP needs nothing new and one that speaks MCP
      needs nothing else. What an agent does with these, recorded against a
      real server, is <a href={`${base}agents/`}>the agents page</a>; editor
      setup is on the <a href={`${base}tools/editor/`}>editors page</a>.
    </p>
    <dl class="evidence">
      <dt>lifted by</dt>
      <dd>{mcp.provenance.generator} from {mcp.catalogue.cite}, at {mcp.provenance.commit}</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <style>
    section {
      padding-block: clamp(1.2rem, 2.5vw, 2rem);
    }

    .head {
      padding-block-start: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    }

    .head h1 {
      font-size: var(--step-4);
      margin-block: 0.5rem 0.8rem;
    }

    .lede {
      color: var(--graphite);
      max-width: 62ch;
    }

    h2 {
      font-size: var(--step-2);
      margin-block-end: 0.6rem;
    }

    .sub {
      color: var(--graphite);
      max-width: 62ch;
      margin-block-end: 1rem;
    }

    .note {
      color: var(--mute);
    }

    /* One card per tool: the name, the command it becomes, the sentence the
     * server sends, then the schema as a table. The command sits beside the
     * name because "a shell around the same binary" is the claim, and a reader
     * should be able to check it without opening anything. */
    .tools {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: 1rem;
    }

    .tool {
      border: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
      border-radius: 2px;
      padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
      background: var(--raise);
    }

    .tool-head {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      align-items: baseline;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: 0.3rem 1rem;
      margin-block-end: 0.4rem;
    }

    .tool h3 {
      font-size: var(--step-1);
      margin: 0;
    }

    .runs {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--step--2);
      color: var(--graphite);
    }

    .args {
      width: 100%;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      font-size: var(--step--1);
      color: var(--graphite);
    }

    .args th {
      text-align: start;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--step--2);
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--mute);
      font-weight: 500;
      padding-block-end: 0.3rem;
      border-block-end: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
    }

    .args td {
      padding-block: 0.4rem;
      border-block-end: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
      vertical-align: top;
    }

    .args tr:last-child td {
      border-block-end: 0;
      padding-block-end: 0;
    }

    .cols {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr));
      gap: clamp(1.2rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .methods {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: 0.6rem;
    }

    .methods li {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 0.3rem 1rem;
      padding-block-end: 0.55rem;
      border-block-end: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
    }

    .where {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--step--2);
      color: var(--mute);
      margin-inline-start: auto;
    }

    .next {
      padding-block-end: 3rem;
    }

    .hero-cta {
      display: flex;
      gap: 0.8rem;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      margin-block-end: 1.5rem;
    }
  </style>
</Page>
